    "habitat": "White-oak-group oaks reach every US region: Q. alba, Q. montana and Q. muehlenbergii through the Northeast and Southeast; Q. macrocarpa and Q. bicolor across the Midwest; Q. gambelii and Q. arizonica in the Southwest; Q. lobata and Q. douglasii in California's valleys and foothills; and Q. garryana from southern California north through Oregon and Washington to Vancouver Island. Most are upland trees of dry slopes, ridges, ravines and oak-hickory or oak-savanna edge, though Q. bicolor and the swamp chestnut oaks hold bottomland and stream terraces. Street and park oaks fruit heavily and are the easiest gleaning, but that is also exactly where horse chestnut is planted as a shade tree, so identify the tree standing over you before you pick anything off the pavement.",
