    "parts": "Kernel only, and only after leaching. Identify the tree first: white-oak-group leaves and teeth end blunt, with no bristle or hair point at any tip (NC State describes Q. alba as 5 to 9 deep, rounded, even lobes with no bristles), and the acorn matures in a single season, about 120 days from pollination per USDA Silvics. Shell the nuts and discard any with weevil exit holes, dark discoloration or mould. Then remove the tannins by one of three routes: steep whole or halved kernels in repeated changes of cold water over several days to a couple of weeks; grind to meal and rinse the meal six to twenty times until it is no longer astringent (McClung Museum); or boil kernels in repeated changes of water, about fifteen minutes a change, until the water stops running brown, which is the method the Lady Bird Johnson Wildflower Center recommends. Boiling is much the fastest but cooks the starch, so hot-leached meal suits porridge, mush and pudding while cold-leached flour is what binds in baking. Never eat them raw or under-leached: NC State lists gallotannins in the seed as causing abdominal pain, then constipation followed by diarrhoea. White-oak-group acorns leach in a fraction of the time red-oak-group acorns need.",
