        "tell": "Ignore size - it proves nothing, because bur oak acorns run to about 1.5 in and valley oak acorns are also large. Read the nut instead. A horse chestnut seed is a glossy dark-mahogany sphere, flattened on one side, carrying one broad matte pale-tan scar over a third or more of one face, with no point at either end. Every acorn tapers to a small hard point at its apex (the dried style) and shows only a small circular scar at its base where the cap sat, and its shell is dull tan, often faintly striped lengthwise. Overhead the difference is absolute: oaks have simple, alternate leaves, while Aesculus has opposite, palmately compound leaves of 5 to 7 leaflets radiating from one stalk, and drops a prickly leathery capsule 1 to 2 in across rather than a scaly cup. NC State Extension rates it Poison Severity: High and states it is highly toxic and may be fatal if eaten; poison-centre case reports describe muscle weakness, difficulty walking, paralysis, twitching, hypotension, collapse and airway-blocking swelling after only 1 to 4 seeds."
