    "harvest": "Oak mast is boom-and-bust and species-specific: USDA Silvics gives white oak (Q. alba) good crops only every 4 to 10 years, with years of nothing between, while USDA's Southern Research Station gives bur oak a good crop every 2 to 3 years. Acorns are the winter calorie base for deer, bear, turkey, squirrels and jays, so take a share only in a heavy year, spread the take across several trees, and leave a poor year alone entirely. Gather promptly off the ground: white-oak-group acorns germinate almost immediately after falling in September and October (USDA Silvics), so a short white radicle means a fresh acorn, not a spoiled one. Screen by cutting a sample open rather than trusting the float test alone - a West Virginia University study that dissected nearly twenty thousand acorns found only 56 percent of genuinely sound acorns sank, so about half of good nuts float, while early-stage weevil larvae leave the acorn heavy enough to sink and pass. Treat floaters as suspect, not condemned, and back the test with visual inspection for exit holes, dark staining and shrivelled kernels. Dry the shelled kernels for storage, or leach and freeze.",
