        "danger": "deadly",
        "tell": "The second thing here that can kill you, and the one that exploits the exact cue that makes persimmon easy to find — small orange-tan fruit hanging on a bare tree in a fencerow after the leaves are down. Chinaberry fruit persists through fall, winter and into spring, shrivelling to wrinkled yellow-tan, in the same roadsides, thickets, field edges and old homesites. Tells that work on the fruit in hand, in the season you are picking: chinaberry drupes are 0.4–0.5 in (1–1.3 cm) and hang in loose drooping clusters of many fruits on a branched stalk, while persimmon fruit is 0.8–2 in (2–5 cm) and borne singly (occasionally two or three) close against the twig. Ripe chinaberry has no calyx; persimmon keeps a leathery four-lobed calyx clamped at the stem end and usually comes off the tree with it still attached. Break one open — chinaberry is thin sticky mucilaginous flesh over a single hard, round, ridged stone; persimmon is abundant soft orange pulp around up to eight large flat brown seeds. Whole-tree tells: chinaberry leaves are enormous (12–24 in) and twice-pinnately compound with toothed leaflets, against persimmon's simple, oval, untoothed, dark glossy leaves, and persimmon bark is thick and blocky in small square plates. Note that chinaberry's leaves are ALTERNATE and twice-pinnate while buckeye's are OPPOSITE and palmate — two different deadly trees, two different leaves, and neither is persimmon's simple oval. Toxicity is tetranortriterpene (limonoid) neurotoxins concentrated in the fruit: most US poison-centre exposures are young children and stay minor, but human poisoning is documented at roughly six to nine fruits — vomiting, bloody diarrhoea, weakness, seizures, respiratory distress, paralysis — and deaths are reported in the clinical literature. Where the published tell fails: chinaberry is a warm-climate naturalised tree, running roughly from Virginia and southern Maryland south through Florida and west through Oklahoma and Texas, with scattered outliers further north. Through the Midwest and Northeast it is effectively absent, so this is a Southeast and south-central risk rather than a range-wide one — which is a reason to be sure of your latitude, not a reason to relax where it grows, and note that buckeye covers the northern ground chinaberry does not. For any suspected ingestion call Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) rather than waiting for symptoms."
