        "danger": "toxic",
        "tell": "Round yellow, orange or red berries in the same abandoned pastures, fencerows and roadsides, ripening and persisting through the same autumn months, all carrying solanine-type glycoalkaloids: burning mouth, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and with a real dose drowsiness, confusion and slowed breathing, with children the usual casualties. The structural tell holds all season and needs no flowers: these are herbs and vines, never trees. Horsenettle is a knee-high prickly herb with lobed, star-shaped leaves and spines on stem and leaf midribs, its smooth yellow berries 0.4–0.6 in shrivelling on the dead stalk into winter. Tropical soda apple (Florida and the Gulf states, especially pasture) is a viciously spiny herb 3–6 ft with mottled green fruit ripening yellow at about 1 in. Climbing nightshade is a twining vine through fencerow brush with red ovoid berries under 0.5 in. In every case the berry is thin-skinned with dozens of tiny flat seeds in wet pulp, sitting in a green star-shaped calyx; persimmon is a soft 0.8–2 in berry on a woody tree with up to eight large flat brown seeds and a leathery four-lobed calyx."
