        "tell": "Not something you would gather off the ground on purpose, but it is what most often pulls a beginner across a field in November, and it is the one lookalike that can end up in your container without you walking to it: a woody vine climbing into a fencerow tree — very often into the persimmon itself — hung with orange-yellow fruit on bare stems long after leaf fall. Shake that tree and bittersweet lands on your sheet with the persimmons, so sort the sheet by hand. In hand there is no ambiguity. Bittersweet fruits are 0.25–0.35 in capsules packed in clusters along the stem, each splitting into three orange-yellow valves that expose a scarlet fleshy aril; persimmon is a single soft berry 0.8–2 in with a four-lobed calyx and never splits. The berries cause vomiting and diarrhoea and are implicated in more serious poisoning in children, usually via dried ornamental wreaths. Before you walk to a tree, check what is holding the fruit — a woody twig or a twining vine."
