        "tell": "Harmless in itself, but it teaches the wrong lesson. Planted throughout the Southeast and persisting for decades at old homesites — precisely where wild persimmon also grows — with fruit 2.5–4 in, often flattened or acorn-shaped, few or no seeds, a large four-lobed calyx spreading flat like a saucer, and much larger, broader leaves than the native. Non-astringent cultivars (Fuyu type) are eaten crisp and hard, so anyone whose only persimmon experience is a supermarket Fuyu will bite a firm native and get a mouthful of tannin they will not forget. Astringent cultivars (Hachiya type) must go completely jelly-soft exactly like the native, and D. kaki is the species behind most published bezoar cases, so it carries the same load. The rule for anything in this genus that is not a known non-astringent cultivar: soft or nothing."
