    "harvest": "Gather windfalls. Shaking or picking firm fruit only gets you astringent fruit and wastes it, and a single tree ripens over three or four weeks, so the method that works is returning to the same tree every few days through the drop rather than clearing it once. Shake only if the fruit still hanging is already jelly-soft, lay a sheet if you do, and expect to sort.\n\nRUN THE FRUIT CHECK BEFORE ANYTHING GOES IN THE BUCKET. Gathering windfalls is exactly where the buckeye mistake happens, and no leaf tell can save you on the ground — a fallen fruit has no leaves attached to read — so the check has to live in the fruit itself. For every fruit you did not personally watch come off a persimmon-leafed twig: it must be 0.8–2 in, round, thin-skinned and smooth, not a 1–3 in globe or pear with a thick leathery rind; it must still carry the leathery four-lobed calyx clamped at the stem end, where an Aesculus capsule has no calyx at all and only a bare stalk scar; and it must show no seams, because a buckeye capsule parts along three clean seams into three tidy valves while a persimmon has none. Do not sort by squeezing. A green buckeye capsule dents under the thumb exactly like a ripening persimmon, so firmness is a ripeness test and never an identification. Then cut one through as a positive check: cut the first fruit of the day as a matter of course, and cut any fruit you are the least unsure of. Persimmon is thin skin over abundant soft orange pulp holding up to eight flat brown seeds the size of a thumbnail; a buckeye is a leathery rind peeling off one to three smooth seeds that the blade drives straight into as solid ivory, with no pulp and no flat seeds. If a single fruit does not cut like a persimmon, cut the rest one at a time or tip the whole bucket out — and never mill, mash or pulp fruit you have not opened and looked inside.\n\nTHE SHEET GETS THE SAME TREATMENT, and it catches more than the tree you shook. Bittersweet climbs up into fencerow persimmons, so a shake drops its 0.25–0.35 in three-valved orange capsules with scarlet arils onto the sheet along with the fruit; a chinaberry or a buckeye standing over or beside the tree will drop onto it too, and windfalls already lying on the ground land under whatever else overhangs them. Sort the sheet by hand and let nothing through that is not 0.8–2 in, calyx-on, soft and single.\n\nSort on the spot: keep fruit that is soft, whole and smells sweet, and leave anything split and weeping, sour or alcoholic-smelling, fuzzy with mould, or busy with fruit flies — persimmon on the ground ferments fast, which is what the drunk-opossum stories are about — then rinse what you keep before it reaches the kitchen.\n\nLate fruit is a southern tail, not a season. The peak drop is September through November across the cited range, which is what this entry's months show; NC State does record fruit displaying into December and hanging on the tree after the leaves are down, and a mild-winter tree in the Deep South can still be carrying sweet fruit then. Treat anything you find that late with more suspicion, not less: December sits deep inside chinaberry's persistence window, when its shrivelled yellow-tan drupes hang on bare branches in the same fencerows and there are no leaves left overhead to tell the two trees apart. The cluster, calyx and cut-open checks above are the whole of your protection once the canopy is gone.\n\nMind where you pick: persimmon's favourite ground (fencerows, roadsides, railroad rights-of-way, old farmsteads) is also the ground most likely to be sprayed or to carry historical pesticide residue, so stay well back from road surfaces and off anything under a spray programme. The species is not scarce, but it is a keystone autumn food — opossum, raccoon, grey and red fox, coyote, white-tailed deer, wild turkey and black bear all live on it through the fall, and it shows up in their scat all season — so clear part of the drop under any one tree and move on rather than stripping it. Remember the tree is dioecious and root-suckering: only females fruit, a whole thicket can be one barren clone, and a reliable female is worth knowing, protecting and not over-picking. Ask permission on land you do not own; many state parks and public lands limit or prohibit collection.",
