        "name": "Canada moonseed (Menispermum canadense)",
        "danger": "deadly",
        "tell": "A twining vine — no tendrils, no adhesive pads — with large 4–8 in leaves that are broadly heart-shaped to shallowly 3–7 lobed with a SMOOTH, untoothed margin and a stalk attached just inside the leaf edge rather than at the notch. Nothing about the foliage resembles beach plum's small, finely toothed, alternate leaves on a woody shrub. The fruit is the problem: dull blue-black with a bloom, about 1/3 in, ripening August into September in a loose branched hanging cluster, and it is the classic deadly wild-grape lookalike. The standard grape tell also separates it from plum, but you have to read it correctly: moonseed's single seed is a thin flattened disc notched into a crescent or ring, and its pulp is scant and bitter; a plum stone is a thick, plump, hard, almond-shaped pit filling the fruit. 'One seed' alone is NOT the tell — the shape is. On range, be honest with yourself: moonseed is mostly a plant of moist woods, thickets, streambanks and fencerows inland, and it is uncommon on the outer coast, but Go Botany records it as disjunct on Cape Cod, which is the busiest beach-plum ground in the country, and it climbs into shrub thickets on the back side of dunes and along sandy lanes. Dauricine and related alkaloids; ingestion has been fatal. Do not pick from a tangle by feel, and do not let a vine draped over a plum bush go unlooked-at."
