        "tell": "Big invasive shrubs to small trees of coastal thickets, dune edges, swales and roadsides through the northern half of the range. This is the arrangement match that matters: pea-sized round black drupes, ripe from late August into September, in small tight clusters in the LEAF AXILS along the twig — the same position as a beach plum. Checks that work while the fruit is on the plant: common buckthorn leaves are sub-opposite with 3–5 pairs of veins that curve strongly forward and run parallel to the leaf margin toward the tip, and most twigs end in a short sharp thorn; glossy buckthorn leaves are glossy, untoothed, with 8–9 pairs of nearly straight veins. Beach plum has alternate toothed leaves with straight veins ending in the teeth. Do not settle the question on thorns — old beach plum spur shoots go stiff and stubby and read as spines to a beginner, so thorniness is not a reliable separator either way. Cut the fruit: buckthorn has 3–4 hard angular seeds and juice that stains purple-black; a plum has one flattened stone. Anthraquinones and emodin make it violently cathartic — cramping and watery diarrhoea to the point of dehydration, which is why its old use as a purgative was abandoned. One honest caveat, from UMN Extension's work: emodin levels drop sharply as the fruit ripens and little or none is found in fully ripe R. cathartica, so 'the ripe ones are milder' is technically true and completely useless. You will still be ill, and there are other irritants in it."
