        "tell": "Inkberry is the one that genuinely shares the sand: an evergreen shrub of coastal-plain sand, dune swales and pine-barren fringe from Cape Cod to Virginia, with round black fruit under 1/4 in sitting singly in the leaf axils — the same size, colour and position as a small beach plum. The leaves settle it in every month of the year, including harvest season: thick, leathery, glossy, EVERGREEN, with a few blunt teeth only near the tip; beach plum is deciduous with thin, dull, finely toothed leaves. The fruit is hard and dry rather than juicy and stays on the bush through winter. Cut it: 4–7 small hard nutlets, no stone. Saponins bring persistent vomiting and diarrhoea, worst in children — rarely dangerous to an adult, reliably miserable, and enough of them will land you on a drip."
