        "tell": "Not a fruit lookalike — the plant you will actually put your hands and forearms into. It is the most abundant thing sharing dune thickets with beach plum, growing as a low shrubby clump in open sand as readily as a hairy-stemmed vine climbing up through the plum bushes, and by September its leaves have turned brilliant scarlet, which a beginner reads as autumn colour rather than a warning. Three leaflets, the middle one on a distinctly longer stalk, margins irregularly toothed or lobed; the fruit is a cluster of dull white to tan waxy drupes in the leaf axils, persisting all winter, which is also the easiest way to spot the bare stems in a thicket in early spring. Urushiol is in every part including leafless winter stems and roots, transfers off clothing, buckets, knives and dog fur for months, and is aerosolised if dune brush is burned. Long sleeves, and wash the tools and the clothes."
