        "tell": "These are true plums, edible in the flesh exactly as beach plum is, so this particular group of mix-ups costs you flavour rather than health — the deadly members of the genus are listed separately above. Beach plum is a low, mounded, wind-sheared shrub, usually knee to shoulder high (to 6–8 ft only in shelter), rooted in loose sand within sight of salt water and spreading in root-suckering clones. American and Canada plum are upright thicket-forming shrubs or small trees of inland fencerows and river terraces, with thorny branchlets and larger fruit; Chickasaw and flatwoods plum replace them south of the Delmarva, outside this plant's range. Be honest about sloe (P. spinosa), naturalised in coastal New England: it is not reliably separable from beach plum on fruit alone, and the usual advice to look for thorns fails in both directions, since old beach plum spur shoots go stiff and stubby. Sloe is fiercely thorny, its fruit is smaller, harder and more astringent, and it also makes excellent jelly — so if you cannot split them, cook them and stop worrying. One rule covers the whole genus and is not optional: the stone is cyanogenic in every species here. Eat the flesh, discard the stones, never grind them."
