    "habitat": "Deep, well-drained, near-neutral bottomland and cove soils, plus fencerows, old-field edges, roadsides and yards, from New England west to eastern South Dakota and south to western Oklahoma, central Texas and northwest Florida. Those same river-bottom and cove sites are prime yellow buckeye ground - it wants the identical moist, deep, well-drained soil - and horse chestnut is a standard planted shade tree along the very roadsides and yards this entry sends you to, so expect Aesculus fruit underfoot in the same September woods. It will not tolerate poorly drained ground, so skip soggy bottoms; open-grown trees carry by far the heaviest crops. Widely planted well beyond this native range.",
    "indicator": "An open-crowned tree with dark, diamond-furrowed bark standing over conspicuously bare ground - juglone from its roots suppresses plants under the dripline.",
    "parts": "Kernel only. Identify the tree before you pick anything up: alternate, pinnately compound leaves one to two feet long with 15-23 leaflets, dark diamond-furrowed bark, and a fleshy green husk holding exactly one rock-hard, deeply grooved nut - never the opposite, palmate leaves and three-valved capsule of a buckeye. Cut the first fruit of the day through with a knife as a positive check: black walnut is thick fibrous green flesh whose juice stains the blade and your fingers yellow-brown within minutes, wrapped around a single shell the knife cannot enter, while a buckeye is a thin leathery rind over one to three smooth, shell-less seeds the blade slices straight through into solid ivory. The husk is not food: its juice stains skin, clothing and concrete brown for weeks and can irritate skin, so wear rubber gloves. Only once the tree is confirmed does ripeness matter - gather when the green husk dents under thumb pressure, then hull the same day, because a two-week delay in husk removal turns kernels black and rancid. The dent test is a ripeness test and nothing more: a green buckeye capsule dents under thumb pressure too, so it can never stand in for identification. Wash the nuts, throw out any that are mouldy or whose shells are cracked or fissured, then cure them in a single layer one nut deep in a cool, dry, ventilated spot out of direct sun for two to three weeks before cracking. Keep shelled kernels in the fridge or freezer; the oil goes rancid quickly.",
