        "tell": "Habitat and calendar put this one directly under your feet: yellow buckeye (A. flava) grows in the same river bottoms, coves and moist north slopes as black walnut and drops a smooth, round, leathery capsule 1.5-2 in across - the same size and silhouette as a green walnut hull; Ohio buckeye (A. glabra) fruits September-October in the same rich valley, ravine and low-woods edge; and horse chestnut is a standard yard and street planting. Thumb pressure will not separate them - a green Aesculus capsule dents too - so cut the fruit through. Black walnut is thick fibrous green flesh, sharply aromatic, whose juice stains the blade and your fingers yellow-brown within minutes, around one rock-hard, deeply corrugated shell the knife cannot enter. Aesculus is a thin leathery rind peeling off one to three smooth, shell-less seeds - glossy mahogany when ripe, pale ivory when green, each with one large pale scar - that the blade cuts straight into as solid ivory, with no stain and no smell. A drying Aesculus capsule shows three sutures and splits into three valves; a walnut husk is fleshy and never splits. Overhead the difference is absolute: Aesculus leaves are opposite and palmate, five to seven leaflets radiating from one point; walnut leaves are alternate and pinnate, 15-23 leaflets along a stalk one to two feet long. NC State Extension rates both A. flava and A. hippocastanum Poison Severity: High, saying of yellow buckeye that it can cause death in humans, livestock and pets and of horse chestnut that it is highly toxic and may be fatal if eaten; poison centres record severe poisoning - weakness, twitching, paralysis, collapse, airway swelling - after only one to four seeds, with children the usual casualties. No Aesculus seed is edible by any preparation, and no amount of leaching makes one safe."
