    "harvest": "Pick nuts up off the ground within a few days of the drop, never from the tree. Ask permission for yard trees, and skip trees on sprayed lawns, sprayed rights-of-way and busy road shoulders. Spread your take across several heavy-bearing yard or roadside trees rather than stripping one isolated woodland tree, whose crop is winter mast for squirrels, deer and turkey. Discard any nut that overwintered on the ground, shows mould, or has a cracked or fissured shell: Penicillium crustosum growing on fallen walnuts produces the tremorgenic mycotoxin penitrem A, documented in dog poisonings and in one adult man who ate about eight mouldy kernels and developed tremors, incoordination and confusion within 12 hours. Anyone with a tree-nut allergy should not eat these at all - walnut is a major food allergen and anaphylaxis is the one hazard here that genuinely kills. Oblong, sticky-hairy fruits are butternut (Juglans cinerea): also edible, but leave them, as the species is collapsing from butternut canker.",
