    "habitat": "Full sun on ground that was cut, burned, bulldozed or abandoned within roughly the last two to ten years. Brambles are pioneers: they want well-drained, moderately acid soil — around pH 5.5–6.5 for the cultivated forms, though wild canes tolerate thin, poor, gravelly ground — they will not sit in standing water, and they set fruit only where light reaches the cane. That last point decides where you look. A thicket under closed canopy is all leaf and no berry, and even in a productive patch the fruit is on the sunlit outer face and the top, not in the shaded interior. Seed arrives by bird, so patches follow the perches: fencelines, wire, hedgerows, isolated trees, brush piles, the drip line of a barn. Classic ground is an old field going back to woods, a powerline or pipeline right-of-way, a logging landing or clearcut three to five years on, a burn, a skid road, the berm of a gravel pit. Black raspberry takes more shade and richer soil than blackberry and sits further into the woods edge, along stone walls and on rocky wooded slopes. Regionally the genus changes shape: in the Southeast add longleaf pine sandhills and roadside ditches for sand blackberry, and on the Gulf and south Atlantic coastal plain the trailing dewberries run flat over open sand and pasture, fruiting well before anything stands up; west of the Cascade crest and through coastal and central California the invasive Himalayan and cutleaf blackberries own riparian corridors, ditches, road embankments and every neglected lot below about 5,000 ft, but they need mild winters and are scarce in the cold interior West, where you are looking instead at native trailing blackberry and whitebark black raspberry on montane burns and logged slopes; in the arid Southwest brambles are confined to canyon riparian strips and mountain drainages. The company matters as much as the soil, and it is the reason this entry is rated the way it is. The sunlit disturbed edge is also the preferred habitat of poison ivy, wild grape, moonseed, pokeweed, black and bittersweet nightshade, buckthorn, multiflora rose and Virginia creeper, and several of those climb up through the canes and dangle their fruit inside the bramble. A blackberry thicket is a mixed-species tangle by default, not a monoculture with the occasional intruder.",
    "indicator": "Two- to five-year-old disturbance with a sunny south or west face — a powerline cut, a logging landing, a burn, an old fenceline strung with poison ivy, wild grape and multiflora rose. In the Pacific Northwest the pairing is Scotch broom and Himalayan blackberry on a road embankment; in the Southeast, sand blackberry under open longleaf pine with wiregrass and bracken.",
    "parts": "Ripe fruit only. This entry covers the berry and nothing else — the spring uses (peeled young primocane tips, dried leaf tea) are a different identification problem, made off the bare cane with no fruit to go on, and none of the tells below carry over to them. The fruit is an aggregate of many small drupelets, each with its own seed, packed onto a central receptacle, and that structure is the whole identification: split one and you find dozens of beaded segments, not one smooth berry with seeds loose in pulp. Blackberry keeps the white receptacle inside the picked fruit, so it comes away solid with a pale core; black raspberry slips off the receptacle and leaves it on the plant, so the picked fruit is a hollow thimble. For ripeness the reliable test is release, not colour: a ripe fruit drops into your hand at a touch, a fruit you have to pull is not ready, and it will not sweeten off the cane. The commonly printed rule that shiny black is unripe and dull matte black is ripe is a cultivated-blackberry rule — it works on R. allegheniensis and R. armeniacus, but cutleaf blackberry and shaded fruit can stay glossy when fully ripe, and black raspberry ripens through red to a bloomed purple-black without ever going matte. Taste one before you fill the bucket. Red or part-red Rubus fruit is astringent, not poisonous, but do not generalise that into a rule about red berries, which is how people end up eating bittersweet nightshade and baneberry. A few hard red drupelets on an otherwise ripe berry is redberry mite damage, not a warning. No preparation is required for safety anywhere in the US: there is no toxic part, no leaching, no required cooking, and the fruit is safe raw. Two handling notes, neither about toxicity. Wild fruit routinely carries spotted-wing drosophila larvae — a 15-minute soak in cool salted water, about a tablespoon of salt per quart, floats them out, and they are harmless if you skip it. And within the northern range where the fox tapeworm Echinococcus multilocularis is established — Alaska, and the north-central states and prairie provinces into which it is spreading — public-health advice is to cook low-hanging fruit picked at canid latrine height rather than eat it raw. The risk is very low and the geography narrow; it is the only argument for cooking a blackberry that exists.",
    "caution": "expert",
