    "harvest": "How generous to be depends entirely on which bramble you are standing in. Himalayan and cutleaf blackberry are listed noxious weeds in Oregon and Washington and among the worst riparian invaders in the West — strip them bare, and cut canes too if the landowner wants them gone. A native patch is the opposite kind of resource: a mid-August blackberry patch is primary forage for black bear, and for catbirds, thrashers, foxes and box turtles. Pick the sunlit outer face and the top, leave the low and interior fruit, and move on rather than clearing a patch out. Canes are biennial — this year's green primocane carries next year's crop — so do not trample, mow or cut them if you want the patch back. The real hazards here are not the plant. Rights-of-way are sprayed: utility corridors, pipeline easements and roadside verges get foliar herbicide from early summer into fall, and a powerline cut in full berry is exactly the site that gets treated. Look for browned, curled or leaning non-target vegetation, skip fruit within splash distance of a road shoulder, and stay off railroad ballast and old orchard ground (lead arsenate) entirely. July and August in a bramble thicket is peak tick; in the Southeast and lower Midwest that means lone star ticks and a real chance of alpha-gal syndrome — a lasting red-meat allergy — from a single bite, so permethrin-treated trousers and a check within a couple of hours beat any repellent. Add chiggers in the South, wild parsnip and giant hogweed at old-field edges (sap plus sunlight equals a burn that scars for a year), and poison ivy running up through the canes at exactly hand height, its urushiol still active on bare winter stems; never burn brambly brush. In bear country, make noise — a person picking quietly, downwind, deep inside a dense August patch is the textbook surprise encounter. Legally, wild berries are among the most permissive things to gather in the US: most national forests allow personal-use picking without a permit, and national parks vary by the superintendent's compendium, with several that flatly prohibit digging plants explicitly allowing berries for personal consumption up to a stated volume per person per day. Check the specific unit rather than assuming either way. State parks and wildlife areas vary. Private land needs permission, and in many states the road right-of-way in front of a farm is not public picking ground.",
