      "Oregon Administrative Rule 736-021-0090 (ocean shore: living seaweed 1 March–15 June, one gallon per day, three gallons per calendar year, no holdfast removal, protected species) — https://secure.sos.state.or.us/oard/viewSingleRule.action?ruleVrsnRsn=311378",
      "California Department of Fish and Wildlife, \"Recreational Harvest of Kelp and Other Marine Algae\" (10 lb wet weight per day in the aggregate; Zostera, Phyllospadix and Postelsia prohibited; MPA and special closures) — https://www.wildlife.ca.gov/Conservation/Marine/Kelp/Recreational-Harvest",
      "Rogers-Bennett L & Catton CA (2019). Marine heat wave and multiple stressors tip bull kelp forest to sea urchin barrens. Scientific Reports 9:15050. doi:10.1038/s41598-019-51114-y — https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-019-51114-y",
      "Washington Department of Fish & Wildlife, kelp in Puget Sound — https://wdfw.wa.gov/species-habitats/ecosystems/marine-shorelines/kelp",
      "Oregon Sea Grant / Oregon State University, \"Bullwhip Kelp (Nereocystis luetkeana)\" — https://blogs.oregonstate.edu/coastaltourism/bullwhip-kelp-nereocystis-luetkeana/",
      "Seaweeds of the Pacific Northwest, \"Witch's Hair, Acid Kelp (Desmarestia)\" (internal pH ~0.5–0.8; destroys other seaweed in the same container; not poisonous) — https://seaweedsofpnw.com/witchs-hair-acid-kelp/",
