    "habitat": "Full sun on thin, dry, sterile, sharply drained ground. It is a light-demanding pioneer that will not persist under a closed canopy, so if you are standing in shade you are in the wrong place — which is also the first thing that separates its ground from a yew's. In the Northeast and Great Lakes: granite, gneiss and quartzite ledge and summits, limestone pavement and alvar, glacial till, kames and eskers, sand plains and pine barrens, and above all old sheep and dairy pasture gone back to scrub, where it makes prickly knee-high mounds among gray birch, eastern red cedar, little bluestem, bracken, lowbush blueberry, sheep laurel and bearberry. On the Maine and Maritime coast it runs over headland, heath and stabilized dune (the fat-coned var. megistocarpa). In the Rockies, Great Basin ranges, Sierra and Cascades it sits ABOVE the pinyon-juniper belt rather than in it: dry subalpine benches, talus, rocky ridges and krummholz at roughly 6,000–11,500 ft, in the open understory of ponderosa, lodgepole and limber pine, through sagebrush steppe and out onto alpine fellfield. In the Southeast it exists only as rare high-elevation and monadnock disjuncts — thin soil around rock outcrops on mountain summits, Kings Pinnacle and Mount Satulah in North Carolina, rocky bluffs in Georgia and South Carolina, high-elevation old fields in Virginia — and is absent from the Piedmont generally, the coastal plain and the whole Deep South. Several of those southern populations are state-tracked as rare; look, do not pick. That handful of protected summit outcrops is the whole southern story, and it is why this entry carries NO SOUTHEAST TAG: there is nothing there to harvest, and what actually answers to the name \"juniper\" in the Piedmont, the coastal plain and the Deep South is eastern red cedar or a planted ornamental Taxus. Critically, habitat can NEVER be used to rule out the deadly lookalikes. Canada yew shares the same limestone ledge, cool rocky woods and shaded talus through the Northeast and Great Lakes — the same landform, one canopy shadier — Pacific yew occupies the same western mountains, ornamental Taxus and savin juniper are planted on exactly the dry, sunny, rocky, well-drained ground you would go to looking for juniper, and Virginia creeper climbs over and through the juniper mounds themselves in reverting pasture and on the stabilized dune, hanging its own blue-black berries at cone height inside the bush.",
    "indicator": "Bearberry (kinnikinnick) mats and pale grey-green reindeer lichen (Cladonia) on thin soil over open ledge in full sun — that trio tracks common juniper from New England granite summits and sand plains to dry subalpine benches in the Rockies. In the East, add the signature of reverting pasture: gray birch, little bluestem, and a stone wall running through it.",
    "parts": "Only the fully ripe cones — the \"berries\" — round, 6–9 mm, hard-fleshed, blue-black under a chalky grey bloom, with a faint three-part suture at the tip, coming away with a gentle tug. A cone takes 18 months to 3 years to ripen, so every bush carries two or three cohorts at once on the same twig: hard green current-year cones, dull green-blue second-year ones, and ripe blue-black ones. Take only the ripe cohort. Green cones are bitter, barely aromatic, carry the irritant oils at their harshest, and stripping them costs you next year's crop. At high elevation in the Rockies the current cohort finishes colouring in late August and September and the window closes with the first snow; at lower elevations, on the coast and in the southern part of the range ripe cones persist on the shrub through winter, so a December pick from a stand the birds have not stripped is legitimate. No cooking, soaking or leaching is needed, and none of it makes juniper safer — the safety limit here is dose, not preparation. SORT BEFORE YOU DRY, AND AGAIN BEFORE YOU CRUSH. Spread the picking on a tray and go through it berry by berry, pulling out every fruit on a branched or reddish stalk, every leaf and every twig: Virginia creeper grows through these bushes and its berries are the same size and colour, and once dried the two are the same shrivelled dark object. Then dry the ripe cones on the tray out of direct sun for 1–2 weeks until they are leathery and rattle, look the tray over once more, and crush just before use: game, cured pork, brines, sauerkraut, braised cabbage, a salmon cure. Use 3–8 crushed berries in a dish serving four — a flavouring, never a spoonful, and never a daily tea. The oil (alpha-pinene, sabinene, myrcene, terpinen-4-ol) is a gastrointestinal and renal irritant in quantity; the German Commission E monograph puts the internal ceiling at 2–10 g of dried berries a day for no more than about four weeks. Do not use juniper at all in pregnancy — it is a uterine stimulant with a long and lethal abortifacient history — or with kidney disease or active inflammatory bowel disease. Never substitute juniper essential oil for berries: it is orders of magnitude more concentrated and is not a culinary ingredient.",
