        "tell": "THE ONE THAT KILLS — and take the tell off the needles, not the fruit. In the autumn harvest window a yew's aril is not reliably the neat red cup the books show: it starts the season green and hard, a few cultivars (T. baccata 'Lutea' and kin) ripen yellow, and once birds have taken the flesh what is left on the twig is a bare, dark olive-brown to blackish seed about the size and colour of a small juniper cone. Use the foliage, which works in every season on every plant. Yew needles are flat, soft and limp, soft-pointed rather than spine-tipped, spirally attached but twisted into two flat ranks along either side of the twig, glossy dark green above with two dull pale-green bands beneath and NO white stripe on the upper surface; the twigs and buds are green; crushed foliage smells green and faintly bitter, never resinous. Common juniper needles are stiff, spine-tipped and genuinely painful to grip, set in whorls of THREE around the twig, jointed and free at the base, and each carries one broad chalk-white stomatal band down the middle of the UPPER surface; crushed, they smell of gin. If the needles are flat and in two ranks, walk away, whatever colour the fruit is. Everything on a yew but the fleshy aril is loaded with taxine alkaloids — the seed inside that aril most of all, which makes \"the berries are edible\" the single most dangerous half-truth about this plant, and the reason people swallow the lethal part while believing they ate the safe one. Taxine is a rapidly absorbed cardiac depressant: roughly 50 g of foliage can kill an adult, death can follow within hours, there is no antidote, and the classic presentation is few or no premonitory symptoms before the heart stops. Documented human ingestions include a man who ate yew needles over an argument about whether yew or juniper was the more poisonous. Range overlaps this entry completely: Canada yew is a low sprawling shrub on limestone ledge, cool rocky woods and talus through the Northeast and Great Lakes, Pacific yew is in the western mountains, Florida yew in the panhandle, and T. baccata, T. cuspidata and T. × media are the default hedge shrub in every state. Any suspected ingestion is an emergency — call Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) immediately rather than waiting for symptoms."
