        "danger": "deadly",
        "tell": "The published tell — \"savin has scale leaves, common juniper never does\" — is true of common juniper and FALSE in reverse, and it fails in exactly the situation you meet it in. Savin and Chinese juniper bear juvenile awl/needle foliage as well as adult scales, and they keep those needles on seedlings, on vigorous shoots, and above all on shaded shoots low in the crown — the underside of a ground-cover mat, which is the part you have your hand in. Cultivars such as J. sabina 'Tamariscifolia' stay largely needle-leaved for life. Use the needle base and the white band instead, and they work on every juniper in North America. Common juniper's needles are ARTICULATED: jointed at the base, free of the twig, snapping off cleanly and leaving a small peg, and each carries one broad continuous chalk-white band down the middle of the upper surface with a green keel beneath. Every juniper in section Sabina — savin, Chinese, creeping, red cedar, Rocky Mountain — has DECURRENT juvenile needles whose bases run down and fuse into the twig with no joint, usually with a resin gland on the back, and no single clean white stripe above. Second check: crush a sprig. Common juniper smells of gin; savin smells rank and acrid, a turpentine-and-cat note nobody calls pleasant. Third: savin's cones hang on short recurved, downturned stalks, while common juniper's sit on very short straight stalks held close to the twig. GRADED DEADLY IN THIS GUIDE, alongside Aesculus, pokeweed, sheep laurel and chokecherry, and the grade is this plant's own record rather than a precaution. Savin foliage runs 2–5% essential oil and carries sabinyl acetate, sabinol and podophyllotoxin, an antimitotic. Ingestion gives violent haemorrhagic gastroenteritis and renal tubular necrosis: roughly 25–50 g of crushed foliage or unripe cones is reported to have produced fatal kidney necrosis, about 1 g of savin oil — five or six drops — is a reported lethal adult dose, and across the nineteenth and twentieth centuries savin taken as an abortifacient, as oil or as a decoction of the tops, killed the women who took it as a matter of routine, often alongside the pregnancy. There is no culinary dose, no safe amount and no preparation that fixes it. These are among the most-planted landscape shrubs in the country, which gives the practical rule: do not pick juniper in a garden, a parking-lot island, a cemetery or a highway planting — and those beds are sprayed as well."
