        "tell": "NOT A BUSH YOU WOULD MISIDENTIFY — A BERRY THAT ENDS UP IN YOUR BAG. Creeper scrambles over and through knee-high juniper mounds on exactly this entry's ground: reverting sheep and dairy pasture, stone walls, gray birch scrub and sand plain across the Northeast and Great Lakes, and the stabilized dune and headland thickets of the Maine and Maritime coast. It fruits from late August through October, the front half of the juniper window, so both are ripe on the same mat on the same day. The fruit is the problem: 6–8 mm, dull blue-black under a waxy bloom — the same size and the same colour as a ripe juniper cone at arm's length, and the same thing again once dried. Four checks, in the order you can actually use them. ONE, ARCHITECTURE: creeper berries hang in a BRANCHED cluster on stalks that turn coral-red to magenta in autumn; a juniper cone sits singly, almost stalkless, tight against the twig and buried among needles, and no juniper ever bears a red-stalked branching cluster. Do not lean on the red stalk in early September — the cluster stems are still green then and the leaves hide the fruit, and the colour only becomes obvious near leaf drop. TWO, WHAT IT IS ATTACHED TO: creeper leaves are palmately compound, five toothed leaflets radiating from one point on a single stalk, on a woody vine with forked tendrils — never needles. Eastern P. quinquefolia's tendrils end in small adhesive discs, but the western thicket creeper P. inserta has no discs and simply twines, so \"look for the sticky pads\" is not a check you can run in the Rockies or the Great Basin, where that species reaches Montana, Wyoming, Utah, Arizona and California. THREE, SMELL: crush it. A juniper cone smells of gin; a creeper berry smells of almost nothing, green and faintly sour. FOUR, CUT IT: creeper holds 2–4 hard seeds in thin, watery, greenish pulp; a juniper cone is resinous, mealy and sticky-fleshed, with a faint three-part suture at the tip and 1–3 hard angular seeds. WHY IT IS GRADED WITH THE KILLERS: the berries carry needle-like calcium oxalate raphides and probably more, and NC State Extension rates the fruit Poison Severity High — \"May be fatal if fruit is eaten\" — with burning mouth and throat, abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhoea, dilated pupils and weak pulse. Be straight about the evidence: the human deaths behind that line are old, few and disputed. The grade is about the failure mode, and follows this guide's beach plum entry — a handful pulled off a tangle, going into a preparation that cannot be un-made. Juniper is exactly that case and worse, because the product is a dried, crushed spice: a creeper berry dries on the tray into a shrivelled dark thing indistinguishable from a dried cone, and once it is crushed into a rub, a brine, a sauerkraut crock or a braise it is unrecoverable and undetectable. Sort the picking berry by berry before it goes to dry, and check the tray again before you crush. Any suspected ingestion: call Poison Control (1-800-222-1222)."
