    "harvest": "Run the full three-part check on every bush before the first cone goes in the bag — needles in whorls of three, one broad chalk-white stripe down the upper surface of each needle, and a needle base that is jointed and free of the twig — and then run it again on the next bush. Juniper stands are mixed, and a savin or a creeping juniper seeded or planted into the same ledge reads as the same grey-green mat from ten feet away. Then sort what you picked, because the other half of the risk is not the bush but the bag: Virginia creeper runs through juniper mounds in old pasture and on the dune and its blue-black berries are the same size and colour as a ripe cone, so spread the picking on a tray and take out every fruit on a branched or reddish stalk, along with every leaf and twig, before it dries — and look the tray over once more before you crush. Never forage \"juniper\" out of a landscape planting, a parking-lot island, a cemetery or a foundation bed: savin, Pfitzer and the ornamental Taxus that kills people are all standard shrubs for exactly those places, and the beds are routinely sprayed. Common juniper is extremely slow-growing — plants live well past 170 years, and a knee-high mound can be many decades old — and the ripe cones are winter food for cedar waxwings, robins, bluebirds, ruffed and sharp-tailed grouse, which are also the only way the plant moves across a landscape. Take a handful or two from a large stand, spread across several bushes rather than stripping one, and leave the bulk; leave all the green cones, which is next year's crop as well as this year's bad flavour. Do not pick the rare southern Appalachian and Piedmont outcrop populations at all — they are tiny, state-tracked, and the whole southern range amounts to a few dozen sites, which is why this entry is not listed for the Southeast. Get permission on private land. Collection is prohibited or permit-only in most national parks and many state parks and natural areas, and juniper stands on western public land may fall inside a designated research natural area or an alpine closure.",
