        "tell": "The inland one that catches people on the name alone: it is jointed, leafless and succulent, and 'pickleweed' is applied to both plants across the Great Basin and Southwest. Allenrolfea is a woody, densely branched shrub 1–5 ft tall with a grey woody trunk and short plump jointed green branchlets only at the tips; the inland glassworts (S. rubra, S. utahensis) are soft annuals under about a foot with no woody trunk anywhere. Not toxic — the seeds were traditionally gathered and eaten — but the branchlets are bitter, resinous and tough. The woody grey base settles it at a glance, before you get close enough to argue about joints."
