    "harvest": "Pinch or snip the top few inches and leave the plant rooted; never pull it up. This matters more than the usual pick-lightly advice because most of the range's plants are annuals — S. depressa, S. bigelovii, S. maritima, S. rubra all set seed once and die, so a plant stripped before it flowers takes next year's stand off that panne with it. Take the top third at most, from scattered plants, and leave whole patches untouched to seed. The perennials (S. pacifica, S. ambigua) regrow from the base and tolerate tip-picking better, but they are also the ones holding the marsh together. Nothing here justifies a rake, a knife dragged through a stand, or a sack.\n\nWhere you walk does more damage than what you cut. Salt marsh peat compacts and does not spring back; a boot trail across a panne becomes a drainage channel, then a bare scar that persists for years. Stay on creek banks, existing paths and firm edges.\n\nChoosing the marsh is a food-safety decision, not just an ethical one. Halophytes are used deliberately for phytoremediation precisely because they take up and concentrate zinc, copper, iron, cadmium and lead out of sediment, and Salicornia is among the plants studied for it. Skip any marsh below a storm drain, marina, boatyard, wastewater outfall, dredge-spoil site, highway or industrial waterfront. State shellfish-closure maps are the best free proxy anyone has for how dirty a given estuary's water is — read them before you pick. On the Gulf and southern Atlantic in summer, do not wade or handle marsh mud with open cuts; warm brackish water is Vibrio country.\n\nA great deal of this plant is legally off limits. Most US salt marsh sits inside national wildlife refuges, national estuarine research reserves, state wildlife management areas or coastal wetland protection acts, where plant collection is usually prohibited outright or permitted only in writing. On the Pacific, pickleweed marsh is the entire habitat of the endangered salt marsh harvest mouse in San Francisco Bay and nesting cover for the California Ridgway's rail; in the Northwest, commercial sea-asparagus harvest is regulated. Find out who owns the marsh and what its rules are before you cut anything.",
