    "habitat": "Deep, damp, well-drained alluvial soil on floodplain terraces, creek bottoms and the lower slopes of shaded ravines — the flat rich ground a river leaves behind. The Forest Service species review puts it on deep, rich, damp sandy or clayey soils along streams, floodplains and ravine slopes, slightly acid to neutral, never on droughty ridge tops and never in standing water. Canopy is bottomland hardwood: sycamore, boxelder, silver maple, green ash, hackberry, black walnut, honey locust, Kentucky coffeetree, blackgum, and on Appalachian coves tulip poplar, beech and sugar maple, with spicebush filling in alongside. It is the most shade-tolerant tree in that understory and spreads mainly by root suckers, so what you find is not a tree but a clonal thicket — dozens of straight pole-like stems 6–30 ft tall, all one plant, big drooping leaves layered like a tropical shrub. Deep-shade thickets carry leaves and almost no fruit; the heavy fruiting is at the edges — the outer rank of a thicket along the river bank, a canopy gap, a wooded field margin, the lit side of a trail. Native to some 25 states, from western New York and southern Ontario west across Michigan, Illinois and Iowa to eastern Nebraska and eastern Kansas, south to eastern Oklahoma, eastern Texas and Louisiana, and east through the Appalachians to the Florida panhandle; it does not occur wild in the Mountain West or on the Pacific slope, where any pawpaw is planted. One habitat fact is safety-critical and cuts against you: the Forest Service lists Ohio buckeye among pawpaw's stand associates, and yellow buckeye shares the same rich Appalachian coves where pawpaw fruits best, so finding a fruit on the ground in good pawpaw habitat proves nothing whatever about what that fruit is. Habitat does not identify a fallen fruit. Cutting it open does.",
    "indicator": "Zebra swallowtails patrolling low through a creek-bottom understory. Their caterpillars eat nothing but Asimina, so the butterfly means a pawpaw patch within flight range. Then look for the second sign: a bottomland the deer have grazed to bare dirt in which one shrubby thing is untouched and knee-to-head high — deer leave pawpaw alone, so heavily browsed woods often end up as pawpaw and spicebush and nothing else.",
    "parts": "Ripe pulp only — a banana-mango custard, eaten raw with a spoon, or blended into ice cream, quick breads and beer. Nothing else on this tree is food. Ripeness is by feel and smell, never by colour: a ripe fruit gives softly under the thumb like a ripe avocado, releases with a light tug or drops on its own, and smells strongly sweet a few feet away. Understand what that softness is and is not — it is a ripeness test and never an identification. A green buckeye capsule dents under thumb pressure too, so a fruit is never a pawpaw because it felt soft; see the buckeye lookalike for what actually settles it. Green-picked fruit never ripens properly and stays flavourless. NC State Extension is blunt about the rest of the plant — 'Stomach and intestinal pain from eating the fruit skin or seeds. Skin irritation from handling fruit. Do not consume leaves or stems' — the toxic principle being annonaceous acetogenins, and it also records contact dermatitis from handling the fruit. Skin, seed, twig and bark carry those acetogenins at far higher concentrations than the pulp; annonacin in seed has been measured at roughly ten to fifteen times fruit levels. PREPARATION, and this is the part people get wrong: there is no cooking or leaching step that makes any of this safe, because annonacin is heat-stable — baking pawpaw bread does not detoxify pulp that has skin and crushed seed in it. The safety step is mechanical. Halve the fruit, spoon the pulp away from the skin, and push it through a colander or food mill to strip out every seed; do not put whole or seeded fruit in a blender or under an immersion blender, which pulverises seed coat and skin straight into the pulp. Discard skin and seeds; do not swallow a seed whole and do not chew one. Even the clean pulp carries annonacin — Potts et al. measured a mean 0.07 mg/g, about 3.5 mg in a 50 g serving — a mitochondrial complex I inhibitor toxic to cortical neurons, and the epidemiological worry (atypical parkinsonism in Guadeloupe, tied to heavy year-round consumption of a related Annona) is about chronic intake, not a bowl of fruit in September. Eat it in season, enjoy it, and don't make frozen pulp a daily year-round staple. A real minority of people get vomiting, cramps or hives from clean ripe pulp: the first time, eat about a tablespoon and wait 24 hours before eating a whole fruit. Skip fruit that has lain on the ground until it is black, split, insect-bored or fermented — that is where the GI complaints cluster. Wear gloves if you are pulping a bucketful.",
