        "tell": "TWO TELLS. USE THE FIRST WHEN THERE IS A TREE OVER THE FRUIT, AND THE SECOND — ALWAYS — WHEN THERE IS NOT.\n\n(1) THE LEAF OVERHEAD, AND IT WORKS ALL SEASON. Pawpaw leaves are simple, entire, alternate, obovate, 6–12 in long, tapering to the stalk and hanging pendulously in two layered ranks. Every Aesculus has OPPOSITE, PALMATELY COMPOUND leaves — five to seven leaflets spreading from one point on a long stalk like fingers from a palm. There is no overlap and no exception; if the leaves are compound, walk away from the fruit under it.\n\n(2) THE FRUIT IN YOUR HAND. This is the tell that matters, because the recommended way to take pawpaw is to gather what has fallen, and a fallen fruit has no leaves attached to check. Buckeye drops onto the same ground in the same weeks: the Forest Service species review names Ohio buckeye as a pawpaw stand associate, and yellow buckeye (A. flava) shares the rich Appalachian coves where pawpaw fruits heaviest. Habitat cannot separate them.\n\nNEITHER CAN FIRMNESS, AND THIS IS THE THING TO UNLEARN. A green Aesculus capsule dents under thumb pressure exactly as a softening fruit does. The thumb tells you whether a pawpaw is ripe and tells you nothing at all about what species you are holding. Never let a fruit into the bag because it felt soft. Use the three checks below instead.\n\nSIZE AND SHAPE — narrows it, does not close it. A pawpaw is 2–6 in long, most of them 3–5, oblong to kidney- or mango-shaped, often lobed or borne in a small cluster, with thin waxy skin, green ripening to yellow-gold or copper-brown and usually blotched black. An Aesculus capsule is 1–2.25 in across and globe- to pear-shaped with a thick leathery rind — Ohio buckeye's light brown and roughened by blunt spines, yellow buckeye's smooth and tan — and as it dries it shows three seams and splits into three valves, which a pawpaw never does. Note that the size ranges very nearly touch at the bottom: a runt pawpaw at 2 in against a big Ohio buckeye capsule at 2.25 in. Size is a filter, not a verdict.\n\nSMELL. A ripe pawpaw smells strongly and sweetly of banana and mango from a couple of feet away. An Aesculus capsule has no fruit smell at all, before or after you scratch the rind. No sweet smell, no supper — whatever else it looks like.\n\nCUT IT THROUGH. This is the decisive check, it takes five seconds and costs you one fruit: cut the first fruit of the day in half with a knife, and cut any fruit you did not personally see hanging on a pawpaw-leafed stem. A pawpaw is thin skin over soft yellow-orange custard holding six to fifteen flat brown seeds over an inch long, set in rows through the pulp. An Aesculus is a thin leathery rind that peels off one to three smooth, shell-less seeds the blade drives straight into as solid ivory — coated glossy mahogany-brown when ripe, each with one big pale scar — with no custard, no seed rows, no stain and no smell. Never taste to decide.\n\nAesculus seeds, sprouts, leaves and bark contain aesculin and saponins: vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal pain, weakness, incoordination, dilated pupils, twitching, paralysis and stupor. NC State rates A. flava Poison Severity High and says it can cause death in humans, livestock and pets, and the National Capital Poison Center records severe poisoning after as few as one to four seeds. Buckeye and horse chestnut are rated deadly throughout this guide, and historical accounts of buckeye eaten after prolonged leaching are not a procedure to attempt. Rule: identify the tree by its leaves, or cut the fruit open. Colour and squeeze are not identification."
