        "tell": "This is the tree that fools people scouting a patch, not the one that fools them at the picking. All three carry huge simple entire leaves in the same rich coves and bottomlands, and at twenty yards a magnolia sapling reads as a pawpaw. Three checks that all work in fruiting season: LEAF ARRANGEMENT — magnolia leaves are crowded in a whorl-like rosette at the tips of stout branches, pawpaw's are evenly alternate along the whole twig and droop; LEAF BASE — M. macrophylla and M. fraseri have eared, lobed (auriculate) leaf bases, and M. macrophylla is chalky silver-white underneath, while pawpaw's base tapers evenly into the stalk and the crushed leaf smells sharply of green bell pepper and rubber; FRUIT — a magnolia bears an upright knobby cone-like aggregate 2–5 in that turns rose-pink to red and dangles scarlet-orange seeds on threads, dry and hard, never soft and never custard-filled. It is not deadly, but the cone and seeds are bitter and resinous and are not food. Get the tree right in leaf and you will not waste a September trip on it."
