    "harvest": "Pawpaw is picked by touch and by patience, and almost every bad harvest is somebody taking fruit a week early. Never pull a fruit that resists: it will not ripen off the tree into anything worth eating, and you have taken it from the patch for nothing. Take only what releases to a light upward tug, or give a slim stem a firm shake and gather what drops. THEN RUN THE FRUIT CHECK BEFORE ANYTHING GOES IN THE BAG. Ground-gathering is exactly where the buckeye mistake happens, and the leaf tell cannot help you here — a fruit already in your hand has no leaves on it to read, so it needs a tell that lives in the fruit. For every fruit you did not personally watch come off a pawpaw-leafed stem: it must be 2–6 in and oblong, kidney- or mango-shaped, not a 1–2.25 in globe with a thick leathery rind; it must smell sweetly of banana and mango at arm's length, where an Aesculus capsule has no fruit smell at all; and it must show no seams — a drying buckeye capsule splits along three of them. Do not sort by squeezing. A green buckeye capsule dents under the thumb exactly like a ripening fruit, so firmness is a ripeness test and never an identification. Then cut one through as a positive check: cut the first fruit of the day, and any fruit you are the least unsure of, and confirm thin skin over yellow-orange custard holding six to fifteen big flat brown seeds in rows. A buckeye is a leathery rind over one to three smooth seeds and the blade goes straight into solid, pulpless ivory. If a single fruit does not cut like a pawpaw, cut the rest one at a time or tip the whole bag out — and never blend, mash or pulp fruit you have not opened and looked inside. The window is short and moves with latitude: Ohio State reports a three-to-five-week harvest centred on September in Ohio, Kentucky State's pawpaw program runs late August to mid-October, NC State gives fruit August through October, and the Forest Service notes fruit maturing as early as July at the southern edge — Gulf-side stands can start in the last days of July, western New York and Michigan can still be dropping in late October. Fruit does not keep: tree-ripened pawpaw lasts three to five days at room temperature, a little longer refrigerated, so pulp and freeze the same day or eat it that week. Leave more than you take. A pawpaw thicket is usually one clone spreading by root suckers, and clones are self-incompatible, so a hundred stems can be a single genetic individual that will never set fruit without another clone nearby and a beetle or fly to carry pollen; the Forest Service records natural fruit set at about 0.45 percent. That means a fruiting patch is a genuinely uncommon event, not an inexhaustible one. Take a share from the outer, best-lit stems, leave fruit on the ground for the raccoons, opossums and box turtles that disperse the seed, and if you want more pawpaws, drop a few seeds where they fell — never let seed dry out, it dies. Do not cut, bend or snap branches to reach a cluster; the wood is brittle and a broken stem costs the clone years. Watch your footing and your arms: pawpaw bottoms are thick with poison ivy vines, and you will be pushing through them. Legally, this is a common tree over most of its range but a rare one at the edges — the New York Natural Heritage Program carries Asimina triloba on its plant Species of Greatest Conservation Need list, New Jersey's heritage program ranks it S2, and New York's protected native plant rules (6 NYCRR 193.3) apply to listed species. In those two states admire the patch and leave it. Everywhere else, check the land manager's rules first: many state parks, county preserves and all National Park Service units restrict or prohibit fruit collection, and permission is required on private land.",
