    "harvest": "Birds finish a tree in about three days, so the window is genuinely short and the competition is genuinely real — waxwings, robins and grosbeaks will strip a crop the week it colours, and Amelanchier is a keystone early-summer food for them. Take part of a tree and leave the top, which is the part you cannot reach and they can. Pick fully purple-black fruit that releases with a light tug and leave the red: it will not sweeten in a bowl, and stripping unripe fruit takes the crop away from everything else for nothing. Work from the outside of the canopy, and never haul a branch down to reach fruit — these are slow, often multi-stemmed from one old root, and a snapped leader is years of growth. Clusters that are distorted and studded with orange spots or fine tubes have juniper–serviceberry rust (a Gymnosporangium), which cycles between Amelanchier and nearby junipers and eastern red cedars; it is not poisonous but the fruit is woody and bitter, so leave it. Street, campus, orchard-edge and parking-lot plantings are the easiest picking and the most likely to have been sprayed with fungicide or insecticide, or to be standing in road salt and exhaust — ask the owner or the grounds department before taking from a planted tree, and skip verges on sprayed rights-of-way. The fruit is soft and keeps badly: refrigerate and use within two or three days, or spread it on a tray and freeze it the day you pick. Rules differ by land — many national forests and state lands allow small personal fruit harvest, some park units prohibit collection outright, and tribal and private land needs permission, so check the specific unit before you go.",
