    "harvest": "Hickory is heavy mast for squirrels, turkey, raccoon and bear, and good crops come only every one to three years, so take fallen nuts from several trees rather than clearing under one and leave the bulk of a poor year's crop. Even in a good year only about half the crop is sound - hickory shuckworm, pecan weevil and hickorynut curculio take the rest - so reject nuts that rattle, float, or show a small round exit hole, and freeze what you keep for a couple of days to kill larvae still inside. Skip nuts that have lain on wet ground and gone black or mouldy. Hickory is a true tree nut, the same genus as pecan and the same family as walnut, and cross-reactivity is high: anyone with a pecan or walnut allergy should not eat them, since reactions can be anaphylactic.",
