    "harvest": "Scratch a current-year twig and smell it on every single shrub — but understand what that test does and does not do. It eliminates honeysuckle, holly, buckthorn, nightshade, baneberry, Arisaema, yew and, most importantly, daphne, all of which are odourless. It does NOT eliminate sweetshrub, anise tree, sassafras or bayberry, which are all aromatic. Pair it with two checks that cost nothing: leaves ALTERNATE and untoothed, and ONE LARGE STONE in an oily berry. The smell test also weakens exactly where the draft would send you — below freezing, and on old grey wood, a twig gives up almost nothing, so scrape to green tissue and warm it between your fingers before you judge it. Take a modest share. Only female plants fruit, birds strip a ripe crop within days, and that fat-rich pulp is migration fuel for wood thrush, hermit thrush, veery and catbird — a quarter of one shrub's crop and then move on to the next shrub is the right rhythm, and it also happens to be how you notice you are standing under two different species. Spicebush is the primary host plant of the spicebush swallowtail, so check leaves for the folded-over silk shelters holding a caterpillar before you cut anything, cut a few twigs each from several shrubs rather than stripping one, and leave the leaders alone. Two legal tripwires in the southern half of the range: if you find a colonial, rhizomatous Lindera in a seasonally flooded sandy depression, pond margin or swamp edge in AR, MO, MS, GA, SC or NC — slender unbranched stems rising in a dense clone, leaves drooping, thinner, with rounded bases and a sassafras-lemon smell — that is likely PONDBERRY (L. melissifolia), which is federally endangered, and taking it is a federal offence; bog spicebush (L. subcoriacea), of Gulf-coastal-plain bogs and seeps, is likewise rare and protected in several states. True L. benzoin grows as scattered multi-stemmed individual crowns, not clones. Last, watch what you are pushing through: poison sumac shares these wooded swamps and poison ivy climbs these same shrubs. Get permission before harvesting on land you do not own, and check state and land-manager rules — collection is prohibited outright in most national parks.",
