        "tell": "The commonest thing mistaken for sumac in the East, and in August and September its fruit is rosy-red: flat, twisted, papery samaras (winged seeds) in big DROOPING clusters that fade to tan, against staghorn's dense upright cone of fuzzy drupes. Squeeze one - a samara is dry paper around a single central seed, a sumac drupe is a fuzzy bead. Leaflets are entire except for one or two glandular teeth at the very base, each with a bump-like gland underneath; staghorn is toothed the whole length of every leaflet. Crushed Ailanthus foliage smells rank, like rancid peanut butter or burnt nuts; sumac smells green and resinous. Twigs are stout, smooth and hairless with huge heart-shaped leaf scars - no velvet anywhere - and the bark is smooth grey with pale striping against staghorn's rough brown. It contains ailanthone; the sap causes contact dermatitis and myocarditis has been reported after ingesting bark preparations. Nothing on it is food. It is also the tree spotted lanternfly swarms, so a red-clustered 'sumac' crawling with lanternflies is Ailanthus."
