        "tell": "Bright red fruit held UP in a rounded or pyramidal cluster at the branch tips, in the same edges, clearings and old fields, ripening June to July in the north, around the Great Lakes and at elevation - which overlaps the front of the sumac season. The berries are juicy and squash between your fingers, sitting loose in an open branching cluster; sumac drupes are dry, fuzzy and packed shoulder to shoulder into a solid cone you could hold like a torch. Leaves are OPPOSITE on the stem (staghorn is alternate) with 5-7 leaflets, and the stems are soft with thick white pith you can push out with a twig. Raw fruit, seeds, leaves, bark and stems carry cyanogenic glycosides - nausea, vomiting, cramps and diarrhoea from a handful raw. Do not transfer the reputation of common elderberry to this: black-fruited S. canadensis has a flat-topped cluster and purple-black fruit and is a different plant."
