        "tell": "The commonest gray-green opposite-leaved neighbour in bergamot's own prairie and pasture, and before either flowers the two stands look alike from three feet away - same height, same dull gray-green, same soft hairiness, same squarish stem, same toothed leaf. Crush a leaf: vervain has no aromatic smell whatsoever, and tastes intensely and persistently bitter. The leaves are also unstalked or nearly so, half-clasping the stem, and thicker and more wrinkled, where bergamot leaves sit on a short but distinct stalk. In flower there is no argument - vervain sends up several stiff narrow candle-like spikes of small five-lobed purple-blue flowers that open in a ring creeping up the spike. Not deadly, but bitter enough to ruin a batch and reported to cause vomiting in quantity."
