        "tell": "Square stem, opposite leaves, and small pink-purple two-lipped flowers packed in dense whorls in the leaf axils up the stem - that tiered look is what catches people who have seen a photograph of spotted horsemint and are expecting whorls. The tell is the leaf, and it works all season: motherwort leaves are palmately cut into three, sometimes five, sharp lobes like a ragged maple or hemp leaf, quite unlike bergamot's simple, undivided, toothed lance-ovate leaf, and the crushed plant smells rank and bitter rather than of oregano. The flower whorls are also viciously prickly - the calyx teeth are stiff spines that catch your hand when you grab a stem, which bergamot's never do. Very common on old homestead ground, barn lots, alleys and hedgerows through the Northeast and Midwest. Bitter and unpleasant rather than poisonous, but the sap causes contact dermatitis in some people and it is a uterine stimulant best left alone in pregnancy."
