    "harvest": "Cut the top third of flowering stems with scissors and leave the rest standing. Remember that this is a rhizomatous clone-former: what looks like fifty plants is often a handful of genets, so a patch absorbs less than it appears to. Timing pays more than quantity here - Keefover-Ring's seasonal sampling found the dominant monoterpene peaks in mid-summer, so leaves cut as the heads are just opening are far stronger than spring growth or a tired September stand, and you need less of them. Skip mildewed material: by late summer this species' foliage is very often chalked white with powdery mildew, which is harmless but tastes of it, so pick early in the season, from airy exposed stands, and reject any leaf with a bloom on it. Leave dead stems standing over winter - small native bees nest in the hollow canes - and leave the great majority of heads to flower out and set seed, because this is a first-rank nectar plant for bumblebees, hummingbirds, hummingbird clearwing moths and fritillaries. Take from large stands only, a few stems here and there rather than a swathe, and never from a small or isolated colony; prairie remnants are the last of their kind and several states track their remaining species. Where this plant grows is also where it gets sprayed: county road verges, railroad right-of-way and utility corridors are routinely treated with broadleaf herbicide, and a broadleaf standing in a grass matrix is the target, not the bystander - scan for browned-off, curled or twisted growth nearby and move on if you see it. Stay well back from busy pavement, and get permission before cutting on railroad land, cemetery ground or any private fenceline.",
