        "tell": "The closest visual match to a blueberry in eastern woods: hard, round, deep blue fruit with a convincing waxy bloom, in a small open cluster in August and September. They are not berries at all but naked seeds with a fleshy blue coat, so there is no crown, no skin to split, and a single hard stone fills the whole thing — thumbnail test settles it instantly. The plant is a smooth blue-green herb one to three feet tall with one large thrice-divided leaf, not a woody shrub. Habitat is a hint but not a rule: blue cohosh wants rich, moist, often calcareous deciduous woods, roughly the opposite chemistry to a blueberry barren, but highbush blueberry and blue cohosh can both occur along the same damp wooded slope, so do not let ground alone decide it. Methylcytisine and saponins — vomiting, abdominal pain, racing heart; children have been poisoned by the seeds."
