    "harvest": "PICK BY HAND, BERRY BY BERRY, INTO A SHALLOW CONTAINER. The commercial barrens are cut with a blueberry rake, and the folk explanation that raking 'damages the rhizomes' is simply not true — Maine barrens are pruned to the ground by burning or mowing every other year and come back from exactly those rhizomes. The real reasons a forager should not rake are better ones, and the last is a safety reason rather than a quality one: a rake takes green fruit, leaves and twigs along with the ripe berries; it bruises fruit that then moulds within a day; and sheep laurel grows mixed right into the blueberry mat, so raked or fist-stripped pickings carry grayanotoxin leaf — the deadly-rated plant in the lookalike list — straight into the kitchen, where cooking will not destroy it. SORT BEFORE THE FRUIT GOES ANYWHERE NEAR A POT: spread the picking in a single layer on a tray and take out every leaf, twig and dry capsule. Laurel leaf is leathery, whorled in threes and pale beneath; blueberry leaf is thin, alternate and green both sides. Wear gloves and long sleeves on the bog rim and in old-pasture edges, and wash your hands with soap before eating out of the container — poison sumac stands in the highbush swamps and poison ivy in the lowbush edges, and urushiol travels from a pushed-aside branch to your hands to the fruit. Read the two-year cycle before you decide a place is barren of fruit: a field pruned this year crops next year, so an empty-looking barren is usually a pruned one and the next ridge over will be loaded. Leave a real share. Blueberries are a major late-summer food for black bear, and a bear already working a patch is a reason to leave the whole field, not to work the far end of it. Access is the practical constraint in the Northeast — nearly all of Maine's barrens are privately owned or leased and posted, and taking berries from them without the owner's permission is theft rather than foraging. Several national and state park units, Acadia among them, do allow berry picking in personal quantities, but that permission is granted unit by unit, so check the specific park, forest or refuge; tribal lands and state-managed barrens run their own permit systems.",
